# PDFQ from Elixir

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:req, "~> 0.7.5"},
  {:kino, "~> 0.19.1"}
])
```

## Setup

Start PDFQ as described in [`examples/clients/README.md`](../README.md), then set its URL.

```elixir
base_url_input = Kino.Input.text("PDFQ URL", default: "http://localhost:3000")
```

```elixir
pdfq = Req.new(base_url: Kino.Input.read(base_url_input), retry: false)
Req.get!(pdfq, url: "/health").body
```

## Sync compile from a template

`POST /compile` with a JSON body: the mounted `invoice` template and its data (`examples/invoice/data.json`).

```elixir
data = %{
  number: "F-2026-001",
  date: "2026-11-07",
  seller: %{name: "Example SAS", address: "1 rue de la Paix, 75002 Paris"},
  client: %{name: "ACME", address: "42 avenue des Champs, 69000 Lyon"},
  items: [
    %{label: "Consulting", qty: 3, price: 650},
    %{label: "Support plan", qty: 1, price: 199}
  ]
}

resp = Req.post!(pdfq, url: "/compile", json: %{template: "invoice", data: data})
invoice = resp.body

Kino.Layout.grid([
  Kino.Markdown.new(
    "**#{resp.status}**: #{byte_size(invoice)} bytes, #{hd(resp.headers["x-pdfq-pages"])} page(s)"
  ),
  Kino.Download.new(fn -> invoice end, filename: "invoice.pdf", label: "invoice.pdf")
])
```

## Compile uploaded Typst source

Multipart upload: the part's filename is the file's path in the project. `input.<key>` fields become `sys.inputs.<key>`.

```elixir
source = ~S"""
= Hello #sys.inputs.at("who", default: "world")!
Compiled from Livebook.
"""

resp =
  Req.post!(pdfq,
    url: "/compile",
    form_multipart: [
      file: {source, filename: "main.typ", content_type: "text/plain"},
      "input.who": "Livebook"
    ]
  )

hello = resp.body
Kino.Download.new(fn -> hello end, filename: "hello.pdf", label: "hello.pdf (#{byte_size(hello)} bytes)")
```

## Errors

A broken document fails with `422` and a JSON body: `{"error": {"kind", "message", "diagnostics"}}`.

```elixir
resp =
  Req.post!(pdfq,
    url: "/compile",
    form_multipart: [file: {"#let total = (1, 2\n= Title\n", filename: "main.typ"}]
  )

%{"error" => error} = resp.body
IO.puts("HTTP #{resp.status} #{error["kind"]}: #{error["message"]}")

error["diagnostics"]
|> Enum.map(&Map.take(&1, ["severity", "file", "line", "column", "message"]))
|> Kino.DataTable.new(keys: ["severity", "file", "line", "column", "message"])
```

## Async job with polling

`POST /jobs` answers `202` with `job_id` and `status_url`. Poll the status until it leaves `queued`/`running`, then fetch `/jobs/{id}/pdf`.

```elixir
%{status: 202, body: %{"job_id" => job_id, "status_url" => status_url}} =
  Req.post!(pdfq,
    url: "/jobs",
    form_multipart: [file: {"= Async job\nFetched by polling.\n", filename: "main.typ"}]
  )

job =
  Stream.repeatedly(fn ->
    Process.sleep(500)
    Req.get!(pdfq, url: status_url).body
  end)
  |> Enum.find(&(&1["status"] not in ["queued", "running"]))

IO.puts("job #{job_id}: #{job["status"]}")
%{status: 200, body: polled} = Req.get!(pdfq, url: status_url <> "/pdf")
Kino.Download.new(fn -> polled end, filename: "polled.pdf", label: "polled.pdf (#{byte_size(polled)} bytes)")
```

## Webhook signature verification

With `callback_url`, PDFQ POSTs the result with `x-pdfq-timestamp` and `x-pdfq-signature: sha256=<hex HMAC-SHA256(secret, "<timestamp>.<body>")>`. A receiver (e.g. a Phoenix controller, reading the raw body) checks it like this:

```elixir
defmodule PdfqWebhook do
  @max_age 300

  @doc "Returns true for a fresh, correctly signed delivery. Never raises."
  def verify(secret, timestamp, signature, body) do
    with {ts, ""} <- Integer.parse(timestamp || ""),
         true <- abs(System.os_time(:second) - ts) <= @max_age,
         true <- is_binary(signature) do
      expected = "sha256=" <> sign(secret, timestamp, body)
      # Constant-time compare (OTP 25+); it raises on different lengths.
      byte_size(signature) == byte_size(expected) and :crypto.hash_equals(signature, expected)
    else
      _ -> false
    end
  end

  def sign(secret, timestamp, body) do
    :crypto.mac(:hmac, :sha256, secret, [timestamp, ".", body])
    |> Base.encode16(case: :lower)
  end
end
```

Self-check: sign a body like PDFQ does, then verify it.

```elixir
secret = "dev-secret"
ts = Integer.to_string(System.os_time(:second))
body = "%PDF-1.7 ..."
signature = "sha256=" <> PdfqWebhook.sign(secret, ts, body)

%{
  valid: PdfqWebhook.verify(secret, ts, signature, body),
  wrong_secret: PdfqWebhook.verify("other", ts, signature, body),
  tampered_body: PdfqWebhook.verify(secret, ts, signature, body <> "x"),
  stale: PdfqWebhook.verify(secret, "1700000000", signature, body)
}
```
